ader_path_psalms_002_d52f47369f4c6eb6b9c86a38","reader_idea_id":"reader_idea_55538e3591004f25","reader_idea_label":"Psalms 1 and 2 form an untitled gateway that places Torah-shaped flourishing, the way of the wicked, rebellious nations, YHWH's…","synthesis_id":"syn_surrounding_passages_209d03be3d8c"}],"path_classes":["priority"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_psalms_002_d52f47369f4c6eb6b9c86a38"],"reader_idea_ids":["reader_idea_55538e3591004f25"],"section_index":2,"synthesis_ids":["syn_surrounding_passages_209d03be3d8c"]}]},"payload":{"book":"Psalms","chapter":2,"evidence_availability":"AVAILABLE","generated_metadata":{"commentary_prompt_version":"1.8","commentary_schema_version":"1.2","evidence_bundle_version":"1.0","evidence_hash":"3b6de6fbf308c7e5fc552ed1ea4775b0390df86e5c69e0e17ce5543d40ddd0fc","generated_timestamp":null,"model":"gpt-5.6-sol","renderer_label":"commentary-v1.2-five-chapter-structured-enrichment-v1","synthesis_compiler_version":"1.1","synthesis_hash":"7d45444e378435424c46142df63b99f9e8690ac48ca5d1d5a6ed964cc124a33a","synthesis_schema_version":"1.1"},"reference":"Psalms 2","sections":[{"blocks":[{"confidence":"high","evidence_ids":["psalm-2-nations-kings-rebellion"],"id":"block_1","interpretation_level":"fact","synthesis_ids":["syn_historical_context_027daede82c4"],"text":"The opening pictures nations, peoples, kings, and rulers joining in revolt against Jehovah and his anointed. Their desire to break “bonds” and cast away “cords” presents Jehovah’s rule as a yoke they want to throw off. The conflict is therefore not merely between earthly rulers: their coalition directly challenges the authority of Jehovah and the king he has chosen.","verse_refs":["Psalms 2:1-3"]}],"kind":"historical_context","title":"The rulers’ revolt"},{"blocks":[{"confidence":"low","evidence_ids":["psalm-2-closing-wisdom-warning","psalm-2-royal-ane-idiom-caution","psalm-2-son-decree-inheritance","psalm-2-yhwh-zion-anointed"],"id":"block_2","interpretation_level":"disputed","synthesis_ids":["syn_interpretive_questions_27e3462a2315"],"text":"Jehovah’s response reverses the perspective of the opening revolt: while earthly rulers gather against him, he is pictured enthroned in heaven and declares that he has installed his king on Zion. The king then reports a decree containing father-son, inheritance, and enthronement language. Such language belongs to a royal idiom recognizable in the ancient Near Eastern world, though the psalm does not identify one particular accession ceremony or establish direct borrowing from a specific text. Within the poem, “You are my son” introduces the royal grant: the nations become the king’s inheritance, and the iron rod and shattered pottery portray his authority over resistance. The closing verses then address the rebellious kings and judges themselves, urging them to serve Jehovah, tremble, and seek refuge. The expression rendered here as “kiss the son” is disputed at the lexical and translation level, so that wording should not be treated as beyond question.","verse_refs":["Psalms 2:10-12","Psalms 2:4-6","Psalms 2:6-9","Psalms 2:7-9"]}],"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"The divine decree and its warning"},{"blocks":[{"confidence":"high","evidence_ids":["psalms-gateway-torah-king"],"id":"block_3","interpretation_level":"disputed","synthesis_ids":["syn_surrounding_passages_209d03be3d8c"],"text":"Psalms 1 and 2 may be read as an untitled gateway to the collection. Together they place several major contrasts at the entrance: flourishing shaped by Torah and the way of the wicked, followed by rebellious nations, Jehovah’s kingship, and his anointed king. This pairing allows the conflict among rulers in Psalm 2 to stand beside Psalm 1’s contrast between two ways of life.","verse_refs":["Psalms 1:1-6"]}],"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The gateway formed by Psalms 1 and 2"}],"status":"pending"},"prose_word_count":273,"quality_audit":{"archaeology_evidence_count":0,"boilerplate_detected":false,"boile